WHAT THE HOOK MOVED - the whole diff of the two runs of one binary is 9 lines,
all of them 8-byte no-list mutable images, nothing else in the 134 EXACT-bearing
lines changed:
    NL[R16G16B16A16_FLOAT]  opt=0x00200000 -> 0x00202000
    NL[R32G32_FLOAT]        opt=0x00200000 -> 0x00202000
    XMC[NOL]                tail=0x0       -> 0x2000 (stays tiled)
    XMC[DETECT]             tail=0x0       -> 0x2000 (the deliberate-bad-fill row,
                            still EXACT-MISMATCH as designed: the comparison can
                            see wrong data)
    XR64[NOL64]             tail=0x0       -> 0x2000 (stays tiled)
    CEIL[R16G16B16A16_TYPELESS/_UNORM/_SNORM, R32G32_TYPELESS]
                            "no-list: NO UBWC" -> "no-list: has UBWC"

VERDICT
    1. The knob does what its header says and nothing more: it declines the UBWC
       demotion for no-list images whose base block is 8 bytes, and the image
       stays tiled (it does not touch the layout decision, and LST64/SGL64 do not
       move).
    2. A 64-bit image created WITHOUT a list, carrying UBWC, reads through an
       R64_UINT storage image byte-exactly, and survives imageAtomicAdd(+1) per
       64-bit word with every word exactly +1. Same answer with the knob off, so
       the read is not the thing the compression changes.
    3. SGL64 says the same about a compressed 64-bit image WITHOUT any knob (a
       single-member list keeps UBWC today), so the reader and the atomic path
       were already good on a compressed 64-bit image; what the hook adds is the
       no-list shape - the one a class rule would grant.
    4. LST64 confirms the structural claim from the code: naming R64_UINT in the
       list refuses compression whatever the knob does. The question could not
       have been asked through a list.
    So: of the three branches written in the patch header, the run lands on the
    first - "load EXACT and atomic+load EXACT". exp-18(b) has a form that is
    narrow AND backed by a measurement. That is a licence to spend a build on a
    ship candidate and a game run, not a result in itself.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT SAY
    - It is not a compression claim. tail=0x2000 is an allocated metadata plane;
      whether the compressor wrote anything is unmeasurable on this chip with
      counters (see above). A byte-exact read is consistent with compressed and
      with uncompressed storage alike.
    - One shape: 512x512, 1 mip, 1 layer, base R16G16B16A16_UINT, usage with
      STORAGE, filled by buffer->image copy. Clear, blit, r2d and render writes
      into such an image are not exercised; neither is a mip chain, an array, or
      a second view read after the atomic.
    - Nothing about the game: 650.8 MiB is journal 28's byte count of no-list
      64-bit images in the inventory, not a measured traffic share, and the DRAM
      wall in docs/fdperf-a7xx.md is 81 % texture reads. Whether compressing this
      class moves fps is unmeasured, and ADR-0004 (BC + UBWC was not a perf lever)
      and ADR-0008 (no driver-side fps lever for this game) are the prior.
    - sparseImageInt64Atomics=0, so a sparse image with 64-bit atomics is outside
      the coverage of these runs.
    - The tree no longer carries the hook; the binary does (2b61554b26ba...), and
      that hash is the only thing that can reproduce these rows.
